Algorithm:

Step 1: Get the number of meetings from the length of the start time array.
Step 2: Create a 2D array to store the start time and end time of each meeting together.
Step 3: Store every meeting’s start and end time into the 2D array.
Step 4: Sort all meetings based on their ending times.
Step 5: Select the first meeting and set its ending time as the current ending time.
Step 6: Traverse the remaining meetings one by one.
Step 7: For each meeting, check if its start time is greater than the current ending time.
Step 8: If yes, select that meeting, increase the meeting count, and update the current ending time.
Step 9: Continue this process until all meetings are checked.
Step 10: Return the total number of meetings that can be scheduled.

Time Complexity: O(n log n)
Space Complexity: O(n)


-----> Code <-----

class Solution {
    // Function to find the maximum number of meetings that can
    // be performed in a meeting room.
    public int maxMeetings(int start[], int end[]) {
        // add your code here
        int c=1;
        int n=start.length;
        int arr[][]=new int[n][2];
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++){
            arr[i][0]=start[i];
            arr[i][1]=end[i];
        }
        Arrays.sort(arr,Comparator.comparingInt(a->a[1]));
        int curr=arr[0][1];
        for(int i=1;i<n;i++){
            if(curr<arr[i][0]){
                c++;
                curr=arr[i][1];
            }
        }
        return c;
    }
}
